# Reviewed joint operands, 12 September 2026

Document ID: `reiyah.perception-reviewed-operands.checkpoint` · Version `0.1.0` · Exploratory

Both analysts can now receive the same contents-checked admission, with an invertible
projection to common detector identities. The new [interface](../research/perception-reviewed-operands/0.1.0/README.md)
retains the complete review basis and the full compiled joint model. The original open-only
preparer, admission/compiler, matching kernel and frozen study protocol remain unchanged.
The real comparison is still [-8,8]: no human reference interpretation has been supplied.

The concrete deficiency was an open-stage guard that correctly refused a reviewed model, leaving
no common preparation after admission. A separate stage earns its limited complexity by joining
existing source checks and retaining full admission bytes. Its inverse check requires equality
of the entire canonical model, not merely matching interval endpoints. For each shared assignment,
the detection renaming induces a bijection on matchings; unchanged losses and weights preserve
the cohort result. This is conventional graph and source accounting, with no new solver claim.

Synthetic source packages exercise both discovery records, assisted proposals, adjudication,
normalization and preparation. Every supplied world, proposal disposition and source locator is
retained. Two fresh preparations per case agree byte-for-byte; each is checked against separately
selected source identities. The existing Engine and separate certificate checker agree with
direct exhaustive matching from source positions on every finite world:

| Constructed case | Shared weighted enclosure | What the check distinguishes |
|---|---|---|
| Base-neighbor matching trap | [-1,1] | A base-reachable object changes addition value |
| Oppositely coupled anchors, three declared worlds | [0,0] | Joint dependence and an unused Boolean encoding |
| Unknown joint coverage | [-1,1] | No finite-coverage inference from listed worlds |
| Explicitly assumed empty reference | [-1,-1] | Conditional absence differs from missing evidence |

The separate research comparator at `4f6ee31e2f604e4302e2ca0be4d6164a855b11be` agrees on all
four intervals after a bounded, retained test adapter expands the same admitted encodings.
Original common operands remain the source; the adapter is not a second admission path. This
is internal implementation conformance, not external scientific review. A retained adversarial
probe also exposes a comparator-checker limitation: it confirms a report whose preference says
`prefer_augmented` at [0,0] with tolerance 1/10. Its improvement-criterion check does not validate
the separate preference field. That correction belongs to the comparator owner; none of its
source or branch is modified or integrated here. Arithmetic agreement does not close that defect.

All 336 repository tests, 93 measurement tests and the repository research check pass.
Fifteen targeted tests cover wrong selected admission, source/report forgeries, world and edge
loss, weights/tolerance, duplicate-valued row identity, unavailable output, unknown coverage,
wrong-time objects, finite emptiness, changed/resealed packets and bounded-output refusal.
Deleting a finite reference can preserve [-1,1]; the inverse check still rejects it. A first
rules-label defect was reproduced and retained before correction: the new packet inherited the
open-stage label. Reviewed rules now declare their proper stage and bind predecessor rule bytes
while preserving all normalization and loss conventions. The failure and corrected test remain
in the private checkpoint. Exact identities and final validation are in
[verification.json](../research/perception-reviewed-operands/0.1.0/verification.json).

On the small constructed packets, one preparation took 0.195–0.202 seconds; complete packets
were 179,984–195,775 bytes. The complete four-case conformance process took 4.37 seconds and
reported 63,979,520 bytes maximum resident memory on this Mac. Engine/checker and comparator/checker
calls each took less than 0.4 ms, while direct position checks took about 1.2–1.4 ms including
source reads. These warm, local observations ran alongside targeted tests. They measure this
bounded checkpoint's cost, not a speed ranking, real-data forecast or human-review budget.

Targeted primary reading revisited [arXiv:2605.24722v1](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.24722v1),
dated 23 May 2026, sections 3.1–3.3. Its calibration target is an annotation distribution; its
preprocessing includes clustering and an absent-annotation/background premise. Our design
decision is to preserve the original disagreements and explicit completeness assumptions, without
importing that premise. This is a task distinction, not a performance comparison. Exact private
reading bytes and access/redistribution metadata are bound in the verification record. No
third-party payload, training code or method is adopted, and no state-of-the-art claim is made.

